{"language":"FCL-1","records":[{"id":"c1","type":"claim","scope":"conjecture from a short description","text":"The recurring disputes are more plausibly caused by underspecified agreements (each person privately fills in when, how well, and what counts as done) than by unwillingness to do chores.","grounds":"Agreements are reached but later generate disagreement about what they meant."},{"id":"c2","type":"claim","text":"A fixed rota binding persons to days fits poorly with changing work schedules; any static allocation will keep failing, and each failure becomes new conflict material.","grounds":"Work schedules change and the arguments recur."},{"id":"c3","type":"claim","scope":"low confidence","text":"The third friend's avoidance is most directly data about the conversation itself — it has become aversive or felt-futile — rather than straightforward evidence about their stance on chores."},{"id":"cm1","type":"commitment","text":"Process-first private conversation with the avoidant friend precedes any group-level proposal.","action":"Hold a low-stakes one-to-one with the avoidant friend about the process: ask what makes the chore talks worth avoiding and what would make them easy.","consequence":"Reduces the chance that a formal new system triggers further withdrawal; surfaces which possible cause of avoidance is operative.","depends":["c3"]},{"id":"cm2","type":"commitment","text":"Adopt an explicitly provisional, availability-keyed, written task system instead of a permanent rota.","action":"Propose a two-week trial: a shared visible task list where each task carries a one-line definition of done and is claimed against the coming week's actual shifts, with a set date to revise it.","consequence":"Shrinks the ambiguity surface, matches allocation to real availability, and lowers the stakes enough for the avoider to re-enter.","depends":["c1","c2"]},{"id":"cm3","type":"commitment","text":"Pre-agree a no-blame miss rule as part of the trial.","action":"Agree in advance that a missed task is re-claimed or swapped at the next check-in rather than litigated as a fault.","consequence":"Keeps failures logistical rather than moral, which is what makes the system survivable under schedule churn.","depends":["cm2"]},{"id":"cm4","type":"commitment","text":"Treat goodwill as the protected resource: tolerate temporary unevenness while schedules are unstable, refuse silent score-keeping, and raise fairness openly if that is the real issue.","action":"Absorb some week-to-week unevenness deliberately and voice fairness concerns explicitly rather than keeping a private ledger.","consequence":"Protects the friendship, which is what the recurring arguments are actually spending."},{"id":"p1","type":"problem","text":"Unknown why the friend avoids the conversations — overload, conflict-aversion, disengagement, or quietly doing less and dodging accountability; each implies a different remedy."},{"id":"p2","type":"problem","text":"Unknown whether one person is systematically doing more work; perceived unfairness commonly drives chore conflict but is not established here."},{"id":"o1","type":"objection","target":["c3","cm1"],"text":"The account reads avoidance charitably as conversation-aversion; if the avoider is actually disengaged or benefiting from doing less, a process-first approach postpones the fairness confrontation and may entrench the pattern.","bearing":"If true, cm1 delays the harder conversation and the cm2 trial may be gamed; the objection's own force depends on facts not yet available."}],"uptake":["c1","c2","c3","cm1","cm2","cm3","cm4","p1","p2"]}